## Abstract

This thesis proposes a theoretical architecture for an AI system functioning as global infrastructure for the active redistribution and optimization of human cognitive capital. Unlike intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) that operate reactively within locally bounded learning sessions, the proposed Distributed Cognitive Acceleration System (DCAS) is conceived as a persistent, proactive, and civilization-scale optimizer — capable of identifying knowledge deficiencies across heterogeneous populations, computing optimal learning trajectories under real-world constraints, and continuously calibrating its intervention strategies to maximize the aggregate rate of intellectual development of humanity. The formal core of the thesis treats education not as content delivery but as a constrained dynamic control problem, solvable in principle via a Pontryagin-class optimization over a global cognitive state space. We ground the architecture in three intellectual traditions: endogenous growth theory (Romer 1990; Lucas 1988), which establishes non-rival knowledge as the fundamental driver of civilizational progress; deep knowledge tracing (Piech et al. 2015; Fu 2025), which provides the formal apparatus for continuous cognitive state representation; and distributed cognition theory (Hutchins 1995; Zhang 2024), which repositions AI not as a tool but as a substrate layer of collective intelligence. The thesis develops the Cognitive Bottleneck Hypothesis: that the primary rate-limiting factor for advanced civilizational projects — including but not limited to multiplanetary expansion — is not energy, capital, or raw computation, but the number of humans possessing sufficiently deep and integrated knowledge to participate as principal agents in complex system design. DCAS is proposed as the structural response to this bottleneck.

