## I. Introduction: The Problem of Cognitive Scarcity in an Age of Epistemic Abundance

We live in a paradox. The total volume of human knowledge, indexed in scientific publications, technical documentation, and formalized expertise, has never been greater. Yet the number of individuals capable of meaningfully navigating this knowledge — synthesizing across domains, operating at disciplinary frontiers, building non-trivial contributions — remains vanishingly small relative to the scale of civilizational challenges confronting our species.

This paradox is not resolved by making information more accessible. The internet already accomplished a radical democratization of information access, and it has not closed the cognitive gap. What separates an informed person from an expert is not possession of facts but the architectured structure of knowledge — the dense web of prerequisites, analogies, intuitions, failure modes, and tacit skills that constitute genuine understanding. This structure cannot be transmitted by search engines or reading articles. It can only be built incrementally, through guided sequences of learning that respect cognitive architecture.

The emergence of high-capacity AI systems creates, for the first time, the theoretical possibility of solving this problem at global scale. A sufficiently sophisticated AI system could, in principle, build and maintain a model of each learner's cognitive state, identify optimal pedagogical interventions in real time, coordinate those interventions across billions of simultaneous learners, and progressively reshape the global distribution of human cognitive capital.

This is not merely an educational reform proposal. It is a claim about the relationship between knowledge infrastructure and civilizational trajectory. We argue that the transition from reactive AI tutoring to proactive cognitive capital infrastructure is one of the most consequential design choices facing our civilization — comparable in structural importance to the invention of writing, the printing press, or universal public education.

The thesis is organized as follows. Chapter II establishes the theoretical foundations across three disciplines. Chapter III formalizes the optimization problem at the heart of DCAS. Chapter IV describes the system architecture in detail. Chapter V develops the mathematical core. Chapter VI extends the framework to the multiplanetary context. Chapter VII addresses the principal vulnerabilities and failure modes. Chapter VIII proposes the research agenda.

