## V. The Cognitive Bottleneck Hypothesis for Civilizational Development

### 5.1 Statement

We formalize the following hypothesis:

**CBH (Cognitive Bottleneck Hypothesis):** For any civilizational project P of complexity class Σ (defined below), there exists a minimum cognitive capital threshold C_P such that P is feasible if and only if the number of human agents whose individual cognitive states satisfy hᵢ ∈ R_P — the competency region for P — exceeds a critical mass N_P.

For all projects currently labeled "civilizational" (multiplanetary infrastructure, AGI alignment, planetary climate engineering, longevity biotechnology), the binding constraint is not energy, materials, capital, or computation — it is |{i : hᵢ ∈ R_P}|, the size of the qualified human agent population.

### 5.2 The Multiplanetary Case

The case for multiplanetary civilization is the most vivid instantiation of CBH. Building a self-sustaining Martian colony requires — simultaneously — planetary geologists, atmospheric chemists, closed-loop life support engineers, radiation medicine specialists, autonomous systems engineers, psychologists of isolated communities, power systems designers, food production researchers, and a general infrastructure of cross-domain integrators capable of holding the whole system in mind.

The threshold for a self-sustaining city on Mars has been estimated at around one million people — and achieving that from the point at which the first ship arrives would probably take somewhere between 20 and 50 total Mars rendezvous, or 40–100 years at current trajectories. [SAGE Publications](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1089/space.2017.29009.emu?cf-mal-redirected=true)

The human qualification constraint is far more binding than the transportation constraint. You cannot solve the people problem by building more rockets. You solve it by building a civilization where the number of individuals with R_P-class cognitive states is an order of magnitude larger than it currently is.

DCAS is the infrastructure for that amplification. Its global equity constraint (C5) is not only a moral requirement — it is a strategic one. A multiplanetary civilization that recruits only from the existing pool of highly-educated individuals in wealthy nations is cognitively impoverished relative to one that has unlocked the intellectual potential of 8 billion people.

### 5.3 The Civilizational Production Function

We propose a modified endogenous growth model in which the civilizational production function for advanced projects is:

Y_P(t) = G(N_P(t)) · F(K, E, A(t))

where G(N_P(t)) is a "cognitive readiness multiplier" that is approximately 0 for N_P < N*_P (the critical mass threshold), increases nonlinearly above N*_P, and saturates at G_max. The key implication: civilizational projects exhibit a **threshold effect** — below N*_P, they are essentially infeasible regardless of capital and energy inputs. DCAS is justified precisely because it is the most efficient lever for moving N_P above critical threshold.

