## VI. Critical Vulnerabilities and Limitations

A PhD thesis that does not attack its own arguments is not honest scholarship. We identify four principal vulnerabilities.

**VI.1 The Cognitive Offloading Risk (Adversarial Optimization)**

The most dangerous failure mode is a DCAS that optimizes the wrong objective. If the system is rewarded for high performance metrics on assisted tasks, it will find policies that maximize assisted performance — potentially at the cost of autonomous capability. This risk, documented clearly in the empirical literature, must be addressed at the level of objective function design and not merely through pedagogical guidelines. Our anti-offloading constraint C4 is necessary but may be insufficient; its formal adequacy requires empirical validation.

**VI.2 The Equity Paradox**

Constraint C5 mandates decreasing variance across subpopulations. But interventions that most effectively advance learning in high-resource environments (high-bandwidth multimodal interactions, rich simulation environments) may be unavailable in low-infrastructure contexts. DCAS could satisfy C5 superficially by reducing the ceiling rather than raising the floor. The architecture must explicitly define equity constraints in terms of absolute, not relative, cognitive capital thresholds.

**VI.3 The Epistemological Bias Problem**

The World Knowledge Graph 𝒦 is not a neutral artifact. Its construction embeds epistemological assumptions about what counts as knowledge, what is prerequisite to what, and what has high civilizational relevance. A graph constructed by a narrow team will reproduce the biases of that team at global scale. This is not a technical problem — it is a governance problem that requires institutional solutions beyond the scope of any single thesis.

**VI.4 The Autonomy-Optimization Tension**

A system that optimally allocates cognitive development resources across a population is, in some sense, planning the intellectual trajectories of human beings at civilizational scale. This raises profound questions about autonomy, consent, and the right to intellectual self-determination. We do not dismiss these concerns — we flag them as foundational constraints on any deployment architecture, requiring not only technical but philosophical and legal elaboration that this thesis identifies but does not resolve.

