## VIII. Conclusion: AI as Civilizational Infrastructure

The history of civilization can be read as the history of cognitive infrastructure. Writing externalized memory and enabled asynchronous knowledge transfer across time. The printing press democratized the written word and compressed the timescale of intellectual diffusion by an order of magnitude. Universal public education transformed the cognitive substrate of industrial societies. In each case, the infrastructure preceded and enabled the civilizational leap that followed.

DCAS is a proposal for the next cognitive infrastructure layer. Its aim is not to replace human thinking but to structure the conditions under which it develops — ensuring that no human cognitive capacity is wasted to the accident of birth geography, linguistic marginalization, or economic exclusion.

The thesis does not claim that DCAS can be built easily, or that the problems identified in Chapter VI are minor. It claims something more modest and more important: that the problem it addresses — the global distribution of cognitive capital — is the central strategic problem of the next century, that the tools to approach it now exist in nascent form, and that the theoretical architecture proposed here provides a rigorous enough foundation to begin building.

The multiplanetary argument is the existential version of this case. But the argument does not depend on it. Even in a civilization that never leaves Earth, the waste of human cognitive potential — the scientists who never discovered, the engineers who never built, the thinkers who never thought, because they were born in the wrong place to the wrong parents in the wrong era — is the deepest structural injustice of our historical arrangement.

DCAS is proposed as a partial remedy. Claude Lévi-Strauss once described the engineer as someone who works with what is available and the bricoleur as someone who works with what remains. This thesis is not a work of bricolage. It is a proposal for engineering — precise, constrained, formally grounded — the infrastructure of a thinking civilization.

