5.1 Formal Definition of the DCAS Optimal Control Problem

We consider a population of N learners, indexed i \in \{1,\dots,N\}, with a knowledge graph \mathcal{K} = (V, E) of m concepts. Each learner’s cognitive state is \mathbf{h}_i(t) \in [0,1]^m. A learner also possesses an unassisted cognitive state \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}(t), which evolves under zero pedagogical control. The two states are coupled through the anti‑offloading constraint. The complete dynamics are:

\begin{aligned}
\frac{d\mathbf{h}_i}{dt}(t) &= \mathbf{f}\bigl(\mathbf{h}_i(t), \mathbf{u}_i(t), \boldsymbol{\theta}_i\bigr) + \boldsymbol{\sigma}_i(t), \quad \mathbf{h}_i(0) = \mathbf{h}_{i,0},\\[4pt]
\frac{d\mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}}{dt}(t) &= \mathbf{f}\bigl(\mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}(t), \mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i\bigr), \quad \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}(0) = \mathbf{h}_{i,0},
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.1}

with \mathbf{u}_i(t) \in \mathcal{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^p compact, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i \in \Theta fixed, and \boldsymbol{\sigma}_i a bounded, mean‑zero stochastic process. The function \mathbf{f} is continuously differentiable and Lipschitz in its first two arguments. The pedagogical control is subject to four constraints:

1. Prerequisite constraint: For each edge (k_a, k_b) \in E,
   \frac{d}{dt} h_{i,b}(t) \le L_{\text{pr}} \cdot \max\!\bigl(0,\, h_{i,a}(t) - \theta_{\text{pr}}\bigr).
   \tag{5.2}
2. Cognitive load constraint:
   C_{\ell}\bigl(\mathbf{h}_i(t), \mathbf{u}_i(t)\bigr) \le C_{\ell,i}^{\max}.
   \tag{5.3}
3. Attention budget:
   \sum_{j=1}^m \tau_j \, u_{i,j}(t) \le A_i(t).
   \tag{5.4}
4. Anti‑offloading constraint: The divergence between assisted and unassisted mastery, defined by a differentiable functional \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{h}_i, \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}) \ge 0, must not grow:
   \frac{d}{dt} \mathcal{D}\bigl(\mathbf{h}_i(t), \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}(t)\bigr) \le 0.
   \tag{5.5}

The objective over a fixed horizon T>0 is the weighted global cognitive capital

\mathcal{J}(\mathbf{u}_{1:N}) = \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{j=1}^m \omega_j \, \mathbb{E}\bigl[ h_{i,j}(T) \bigr],
\tag{5.6}

with weights \omega_j \ge 0 incorporating civilisational relevance, epistemic centrality, and equity corrections. Constraints (5.2)–(5.5) must hold almost surely along trajectories. The set of admissible controls \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}} consists of all measurable \mathbf{u}_i:[0,T]\to\mathcal{U} such that the constraints are satisfied pointwise. The DCAS‑OCP is

\sup_{\mathbf{u}_{1:N} \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}} \mathcal{J}(\mathbf{u}_{1:N}).
\tag{5.7}

