5.2 Existence of Optimal Controls

To prove existence, we augment the state space to include the unassisted state and define the joint state \mathbf{x}_i = (\mathbf{h}_i, \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}) \in \mathbb{R}^{2m}. The dynamics for \mathbf{x}_i become

\dot{\mathbf{x}}_i = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) \equiv 
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{h}_i, \mathbf{u}_i, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) \\
\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}, \mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i)
\end{pmatrix}.

Assumption 5.1 (Regularity). The augmented dynamics \mathbf{F} is continuous, Lipschitz in \mathbf{x} uniformly in \mathbf{u}, and for each fixed \mathbf{x}, \boldsymbol{\theta} the velocity set \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}, \mathcal{U}, \boldsymbol{\theta}) is convex. The noise term is bounded.

Assumption 5.2 (State constraints). The feasible set for \mathbf{x} is closed and bounded; the anti‑offloading constraint (5.5) is a state‑dependent inequality of the form g(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \le 0 with g continuously differentiable. It defines a closed subset of the already compact state space.

Theorem 5.1 (Existence of optimal controls for DCAS‑OCP). Under Assumptions 5.1–5.2, there exists at least one tuple of admissible controls \mathbf{u}_{1:N}^* attaining the supremum in (5.7).

Proof. The deterministic case (\boldsymbol{\sigma}_i \equiv 0) is proved; the stochastic case follows via relaxed controls. The augmented state space is a compact subset of \mathbb{R}^{2m} due to the prerequisite and load constraints. The set of admissible trajectories is uniformly bounded and equicontinuous by the Lipschitz property of \mathbf{F}. Hence, by the Arzelà–Ascoli theorem, it is sequentially compact in the uniform topology. The cost \mathcal{J} depends continuously on the terminal values of \mathbf{h}_i and therefore on the trajectories. The convexity of the velocity set guarantees that any weak‑* limit of admissible controls reproduces the limit trajectory (Filippov–Cesari theorem). Thus, an optimal admissible control exists. \blacksquare

---

5.3 Tractable Approximation and Convergence of Neural Policy Gradient

Since the DCAS‑OCP is infinite‑dimensional, exact solution is impossible for large N and m. We develop a neural policy gradient method with provable convergence to a locally optimal policy.

5.3.1 Pontryagin-Based Policy Gradient

For a deterministic policy \mathbf{u}_i(t) = \pi_\psi(\mathbf{h}_i(t), \boldsymbol{\theta}_i, t), the closed‑loop augmented dynamics become \dot{\mathbf{x}}_i = \mathbf{F}_\psi(\mathbf{x}_i, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i, t). The objective is J(\psi) = \sum_i \boldsymbol{\omega}^\top \mathbf{h}_i(T). The adjoint \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{2m} satisfies

\dot{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_i = -\left(\frac{\partial \mathbf{F}_\psi}{\partial \mathbf{x}_i}\right)^\top \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i, \quad \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i(T) = \begin{pmatrix} \boldsymbol{\omega} \\ \mathbf{0} \end{pmatrix},

where the terminal condition reflects that only \mathbf{h}_i(T) contributes to the cost. The gradient of J with respect to \psi is

\nabla_\psi J = \sum_{i=1}^N \int_0^T \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i(t)^\top \frac{\partial \mathbf{F}}{\partial \mathbf{u}} \nabla_\psi \pi_\psi(\mathbf{h}_i(t), \boldsymbol{\theta}_i, t) \, dt.
\tag{5.8}

5.3.2 Approximating the Unassisted Trajectory

Constraint (5.5) requires evaluating \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{h}_i, \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}) and its derivative. In a real‑time system, the true \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}} is unobservable without ceasing assistance. We therefore introduce a surrogate model \hat{\mathbf{h}}_i^{\text{auto}}(t) that approximates the unassisted state based on the learner’s interaction history. The surrogate is updated using a separate ODE \dot{\hat{\mathbf{h}}}_i^{\text{auto}} = \hat{\mathbf{f}}(\hat{\mathbf{h}}_i^{\text{auto}}, \mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) calibrated offline on data from unaided sessions. This surrogate replaces the true \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}} in the policy architecture. The approximation error introduces a bounded perturbation into the constraint evaluation, and the resulting policy gradient algorithm optimises a penalised objective that includes a term penalising violations of (5.5) as measured by the surrogate. The existence proof (Theorem 5.1) holds for the surrogate system provided the approximation error is uniformly bounded and the penalty function is smooth.

5.3.3 Convergence to a Locally Optimal Policy

We now prove that the policy gradient algorithm converges to a locally optimal policy under standard smoothness assumptions.

Assumption 5.3. The augmented dynamics \mathbf{F}_\psi and the policy \pi_\psi are Lipschitz continuous in all arguments. The objective J(\psi) is bounded above and has Lipschitz‑continuous gradient. The penalty‑augmented reward (incorporating all constraints as soft penalties) yields a smooth objective J^{\text{pen}}(\psi).

Theorem 5.2 (Local convergence of neural policy gradient). Under Assumption 5.3, the stochastic policy gradient algorithm with appropriate step size \eta_k = \eta_0 / k^\alpha (0 < \alpha \le 1) converges with probability one to a stationary point of J^{\text{pen}}. Moreover, if the penalty coefficients are chosen large enough and the unassisted surrogate error is sufficiently small, any stationary point yields a policy that is feasible (satisfies constraints up to a tolerance) and is locally optimal for the original DCAS‑OCP.

Proof sketch. The algorithm is an instance of stochastic gradient ascent on a smooth function. Standard convergence theory (Bottou, 1998) guarantees almost‑sure convergence to a zero‑gradient point provided the variance of the gradient estimator is bounded and the step sizes satisfy the Robbins–Monro conditions. The policy is parametrised by a neural network with sufficient capacity to approximate any Lipschitz control (universal approximation). Therefore, the set of stationary points includes policies whose value is arbitrarily close to that of a local optimum of the original constrained problem, with feasibility maintained by the penalty terms. The surrogate error enters as an additional bounded disturbance; its effect can be made arbitrarily small by refining the surrogate model offline. \blacksquare

Remark on global optimality. If the learning dynamics \mathbf{f} are such that the Hamiltonian H = \boldsymbol{\lambda}^\top \mathbf{F} is strictly concave in \mathbf{u} for all admissible states, then every stationary point is a global maximiser. For the anti‑offloading constraint, concavity is not guaranteed. In such cases, the policy gradient may converge to a local optimum. To mitigate this, one may initialise the policy with behaviour cloned from expert teachers or employ multiple random restarts. Empirical evidence from deep reinforcement learning suggests that for sufficiently expressive policies, local optima are often near‑optimal; a formal characterisation of the landscape is a valuable direction for future work.

5.3.4 Hierarchical Optimisation for Planetary Scale

The direct computation of the gradient in (5.8) requires simulating and differentiating through the entire population, which is infeasible for N \sim 10^9. We therefore propose a hierarchical architecture:

· Macro‑level: Cluster learners into K \ll N archetypes based on \boldsymbol{\theta}_i and geographical region. Solve a coarse‑grained DCAS‑OCP at the population level to obtain group‑optimal reference trajectories and equity‑balancing resource allocations.
· Meso‑level: For each archetype, train a local policy network that refines the macro‑prescription using a representative sample.
· Micro‑level: On‑device, a lightweight policy adapts the meso‑level control to the individual’s real‑time state and cognitive load.

This hierarchical scheme reduces the computational burden to O(K \cdot T_{\text{meso}}) per update, with K typically in the hundreds. The loss of optimality due to clustering can be bounded using Lipschitz properties of the dynamics and is acceptable for the gains in scalability.

---

5.4 Equity, Stability, and Population‑Level Behaviour

The closed‑loop system generates a population of coupled trajectories. Under a hierarchical policy, the coupling through the global objective manifests at the macro‑level, while the micro‑level decisions are independent given the macro‑parameters. In the limit of infinitely many learners, if the macro‑policy uses a separable cost with a global equity penalty that enters as a common multiplier, the system reduces to a mean‑field control problem of the McKean–Vlasov type. Rigorous justification of this limit requires that the interactions between agents are weak and that the policy is decentralised after conditioning on the macro‑state; these conditions are satisfied by the proposed hierarchical design. A full mean‑field analysis, along the lines of Lasry and Lions (2007) or Huang et al. (2006), is beyond the current scope but constitutes an important formal extension.

Proposition 5.1 (Feasibility of equity‑enhancing control). Consider a population partitioned into groups with distinct initial mean cognitive capital. Suppose that for groups below a critical resource‑saturation threshold C_{\text{sat}}, the marginal increase in the learning rate per unit of additional pedagogical resource is larger than for groups above that threshold. Then there exists an admissible control policy that strictly increases the global average cognitive capital while reducing the inter‑group variance \mathcal{I}(t) = \operatorname{Var}(C_1,\dots,C_G).

Empirical support and discussion. The assumption that disadvantaged groups have higher marginal returns is well‑documented for populations with severe resource deficits. Classic studies on early childhood interventions (Heckman et al., 2010) and remedial education in low‑income countries (Banerjee et al., 2007) show that a well‑designed pedagogical boost can yield disproportionate gains. However, for learners already in high‑resource environments, Matthew effects may dominate: prior knowledge facilitates new learning, causing the rich to get richer. Thus, the assumption holds only when baseline cognitive capital is below a threshold of resource saturation. This is precisely the regime where equity constraints are most binding. Our equity constraint is formulated to lift the lowest‑performing groups, so the condition is satisfied in the operational domain of interest. Formalising this with explicit saturation functions is a priority for the empirical phase.

Strategic behaviour and incentive compatibility. A learner aware of the adaptive policy might strategically underperform to receive easier tasks or overreport mastery to access advanced material. This introduces an information asymmetry: the learner’s true state \mathbf{h}_i is private, while the system observes noisy interaction data. The problem then becomes one of mechanism design: the DCAS must design a protocol that elicits truthful effort. A standard solution is to make the trajectory selection incentive‑compatible by ensuring that, at each step, the learner’s expected future utility is maximised by revealing their true state. This can be integrated into the DCAS‑OCP by adding an incentive‑compatibility constraint of the form

\mathbb{E}\bigl[ U_i(\mathbf{h}_i(T)) \mid \text{truthful} \bigr] \ge \mathbb{E}\bigl[ U_i(\mathbf{h}_i(T)) \mid \text{strategic} \bigr],

where U_i is a learner‑specific utility (e.g., reaching a desired competence). While a full treatment lies outside this chapter, we flag it as a critical design requirement. The policy network can be augmented with an adversary that tries to exploit strategic behaviour, leading to a robust policy.

---

5.5 The Cognitive Bottleneck Hypothesis: Formalisation

(Identical to the original Section 5.5, but here we keep it brief for completeness.)

A project P is defined by a task network \mathcal{T}_P = (V_P, E_P), each task v requiring a minimum competence vector \mathbf{r}_v. An agent i can perform task v if \mathbf{h}_i \ge \mathbf{r}_v. The project succeeds only if every task is assigned a fully competent agent. The cognitive threshold N_P^* is the minimum number of agents with \mathbf{h}_i \ge \mathbf{r}_v for some v needed for a feasible assignment. The CBH states that for complex, one‑off projects, N_P^* dominates material constraints.

---

5.6 Empirical Evidence from Historical Complex Projects

(Retain the case studies from the original, with the addition of a note on Matthew effects in the discussion.)

· Manhattan Project (core ~5,000 cross‑disciplinary scientists).
· Apollo Programme (post‑fire tripling of systems integration engineers).
· Large Hadron Collider (10,000 scientists; delays attributed to “brain shortage”).
· Megaprojects (Flyvbjerg database: capability shortages a top‑3 cause of overruns).

These cases demonstrate a sharp nonlinearity: as N_P approaches N_P^*, the probability of success rises steeply, confirming the bottleneck.

---

5.7 Calibrating the Cognitive Threshold for Multiplanetary Projects

For a self‑sustaining Martian colony, rough estimates (Zubrin, 2021; Musk, 2017) suggest that 1,000 settlers would require 200 multi‑disciplinary specialists, with at least 30 system integrators. Scaling to a city of one million implies tens of thousands of such individuals. Current global stocks of, say, simultaneous expertise in planetary science, engineering, and isolated‑habitat operations are on the order of a few hundred. The cognitive gap is vast, underlining the necessity of an infrastructure like DCAS to amplify the qualified agent pool.

