Conclusion of the Chapter

The revised mathematical analysis demonstrates that the DCAS optimal control problem is well‑posed even with the coupled anti‑offloading constraint, that a neural policy gradient method can learn a locally optimal policy, and that a hierarchical architecture renders planetary‑scale deployment computationally plausible. Equity goals are formally integrated and feasible under empirically supported conditions, while the cognitive bottleneck hypothesis is both theoretically grounded and historically validated. These results provide a secure theoretical foundation for the proposed cognitive capital accelerator.

Appendix A: Proof of Theorem 5.1 — Existence of Optimal Controls for the Augmented DCAS-OCP

We restate the DCAS-OCP with the augmented state \mathbf{x}_i = (\mathbf{h}_i, \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}) \in \mathbb{R}^{2m} as introduced in Section 5.1. The dynamics are

\dot{\mathbf{x}}_i = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) = 
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{h}_i, \mathbf{u}_i, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) \\
\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}, \mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i)
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad \mathbf{x}_i(0) = (\mathbf{h}_{i,0}, \mathbf{h}_{i,0}),
\tag{A.1}

with a bounded stochastic perturbation \boldsymbol{\sigma}_i(t) applied only to the assisted component. The control set \mathcal{U} is compact. The state space is restricted by:

· the prerequisite constraints (5.2) — which depend only on \mathbf{h}_i;
· the cognitive load and attention constraints (5.3)–(5.4);
· the anti‑offloading constraint (5.5), which can be written as a state‑control inequality g(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i) \le 0 with g continuously differentiable and \dot{g} \le 0 required almost everywhere.

The joint feasible state set \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^{2m} is compact because both \mathbf{h}_i and \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}} lie in [0,1]^m, the prerequisite constraints define a closed subset, and the cognitive load constraint further bounds the admissible controls pointwise.

Assumption A.1 (Regularity).

1. \mathbf{F}: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \Theta \to \mathbb{R}^{2m} is continuous in (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}), continuously differentiable in \mathbf{x} with derivative uniformly bounded by L_F.
2. For each fixed \mathbf{x} and \boldsymbol{\theta}, the velocity set \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}, \mathcal{U}, \boldsymbol{\theta}) is convex and compact.
3. The noise term \boldsymbol{\sigma}_i is bounded, measurable, and has mean zero.

The admissible control set \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}} consists of all measurable \mathbf{u}_i: [0,T] \to \mathcal{U} such that the state trajectory satisfies (5.2)–(5.5) for all t. The objective remains

\mathcal{J}(\mathbf{u}_{1:N}) = \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{j=1}^m \omega_j \, \mathbb{E}\bigl[ h_{i,j}(T) \bigr].
\tag{A.2}

Proof of Theorem 5.1 (deterministic case). Consider first the deterministic case \boldsymbol{\sigma}_i \equiv 0. The proof proceeds in three steps.

Step 1: Compactness of trajectories. The set of admissible trajectories \{\mathbf{x}_i(\cdot)\} is contained in \mathcal{X}, hence uniformly bounded. Equicontinuity follows from the bound on \mathbf{F} over the compact set \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U}: for any t_1, t_2, \|\mathbf{x}_i(t_2) - \mathbf{x}_i(t_1)\| \le M_F |t_2 - t_1| with M_F = \max \|\mathbf{F}\|. Because the bound is uniform across all admissible controls, the family is equicontinuous. By the Arzelà–Ascoli theorem, every sequence of admissible trajectories contains a subsequence converging uniformly to some limit \mathbf{x}_i^*(\cdot).

Step 2: Closure under weak limits of controls. Take a sequence of admissible controls \mathbf{u}_i^{(n)} with trajectories \mathbf{x}_i^{(n)} \to \mathbf{x}_i^* uniformly. Since \mathcal{U} is compact, the sequence \{\mathbf{u}_i^{(n)}\} is bounded in L^\infty([0,T], \mathcal{U}) and hence admits a weakly-* convergent subsequence (Alaoglu’s theorem) to some \mathbf{u}_i^* \in L^\infty([0,T], \mathcal{U}). The differential inclusion \dot{\mathbf{x}}_i \in \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathcal{U}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) defines a system with a convex, compact right‑hand side (by Assumption A.1.2). For such systems, a standard closure theorem (e.g., Filippov’s lemma) guarantees that the uniform limit \mathbf{x}_i^* is a solution of the differential inclusion corresponding to the limit control \mathbf{u}_i^*. Moreover, because the state constraints define a closed set and the inequality g(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \le 0 is preserved under uniform convergence and weak convergence of controls (by continuity of the map (\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u}) \mapsto \dot{g} = \nabla g \cdot \mathbf{F}), the limit trajectory remains admissible. Thus, the set of admissible trajectories is closed, hence compact.

Step 3: Continuity of the cost. The cost \mathcal{J} depends only on the terminal components \mathbf{h}_i(T), which are continuous functions of the trajectory. Therefore, \mathcal{J} is continuous on the compact set of admissible trajectories. By the Weierstrass extreme value theorem, an optimal control exists.

Extension to the stochastic case. When \boldsymbol{\sigma}_i is present, we consider the associated relaxed control problem where controls are probability measures over \mathcal{U}. The space of relaxed controls is compact in the narrow topology, and the cost becomes linear in the occupation measure. The existence of an optimal relaxed control follows by the same compactness–continuity argument. Because the original dynamics are affine in the control and the constraints involve the control in a convex manner, the optimal relaxed control can be realised by an ordinary control (chattering lemma, see Cesari 1983). Hence an optimal admissible control exists for the stochastic DCAS‑OCP as well. \blacksquare

