Appendix B: Pontryagin’s Maximum Principle for the Augmented DCAS-OCP

The augmented state \mathbf{x}_i = (\mathbf{h}_i, \mathbf{h}_i^{\text{auto}}) evolves as in (A.1). The constraints (5.2)–(5.5) are all expressible as mixed state‑control inequalities of the form

G_k(\mathbf{x}_i(t), \mathbf{u}_i(t)) \le 0, \qquad k = 1, \dots, K,

with the anti‑offloading constraint imposing the additional condition \frac{d}{dt} G_{\text{off}}(\mathbf{x}_i(t)) \le 0. This is equivalent to a first‑order state constraint and is handled via the standard maximum principle for state‑constrained problems (Hartl, Sethi & Vickson 1995).

Define the Hamiltonian for learner i as

\mathcal{H}_i(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i, \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i, \boldsymbol{\mu}_i) = \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i^\top \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) + \sum_{k} \mu_{i,k} G_k(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i),

where \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{2m} is the costate and \boldsymbol{\mu}_i(t) \ge 0 are multipliers associated with the active constraints. The costate satisfies the adjoint equation

\dot{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_i = -\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}_i}{\partial \mathbf{x}_i}
\tag{B.1}

with the terminal transversality condition

\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i(T) = \begin{pmatrix} \boldsymbol{\omega} \\ \mathbf{0} \end{pmatrix},

since only the \mathbf{h}_i component contributes to the terminal cost \Phi(\mathbf{x}_i) = \sum_j \omega_j h_{i,j}.

The optimal control \mathbf{u}_i^* must satisfy the pointwise maximisation

\mathbf{u}_i^*(t) \in \arg\max_{\mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{admissible}}(\mathbf{x}_i(t))} \mathcal{H}_i(\mathbf{x}_i(t), \mathbf{u}, \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i(t), \boldsymbol{\mu}_i(t)),
\tag{B.2}

where \mathcal{U}_{\text{admissible}}(\mathbf{x}) is the set of controls satisfying the instantaneous bounds and state‑dependent constraints at \mathbf{x}.

For the anti‑offloading constraint g(\mathbf{x}) = \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{h}, \mathbf{h}^{\text{auto}}) with \dot{g} \le 0, the condition enters the Hamiltonian as a state constraint of order one. The corresponding multiplier \mu_{\text{off}} is non‑negative, supported on the set where \dot{g} = 0, and the adjoint undergoes a jump when the constraint becomes active. The precise jump conditions are standard (Hartl et al., 1995, Theorem 4.1) and are not reproduced in full here; they ensure that the adjoint vector carries the necessary information to penalise control actions that would cause divergence.

These necessary conditions provide the theoretical foundation for the adjoint sensitivity method used to train the neural policy: the policy gradient derived in Appendix C approximates the optimal control that satisfies (B.2).

