Appendix C: Policy Gradient Convergence and Proof of Revised Theorem 5.2

We now formalise the neural policy gradient algorithm and prove its convergence to a locally optimal policy for the penalty‑augmented DCAS-OCP.

C.1 Policy Parametrisation and Penalised Objective

Let the policy be a deterministic neural network \mathbf{u}_i = \pi_\psi(\mathbf{h}_i, \hat{\mathbf{h}}_i^{\text{auto}}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i, t), where \hat{\mathbf{h}}_i^{\text{auto}} is a surrogate for the true unassisted state, updated offline. The closed‑loop augmented dynamics for the surrogate system become

\dot{\mathbf{X}}_i = \tilde{\mathbf{F}}_\psi(\mathbf{X}_i, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i, t),
\qquad \mathbf{X}_i = (\mathbf{h}_i, \hat{\mathbf{h}}_i^{\text{auto}}),

with the surrogate unassisted model approximating \mathbf{f}(\cdot, \mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_i) with a bounded error \epsilon_{\text{sur}}.

The constraints (5.2)–(5.5) are incorporated via differentiable penalty functions added to the objective. The penalised objective for a single learner is

J_i^{\text{pen}}(\psi) = \boldsymbol{\omega}^\top \mathbf{h}_i(T) - \sum_{k} \rho_k \int_0^T \bigl[ \max(0, G_k(\mathbf{X}_i(t), \pi_\psi)) \bigr]^2 dt,

where \rho_k > 0 are penalty coefficients. The total objective is J^{\text{pen}}(\psi) = \sum_i \mathbb{E}[ J_i^{\text{pen}}(\psi) ].

C.2 Policy Gradient

Applying the adjoint sensitivity method to the penalised objective yields the gradient

\nabla_\psi J^{\text{pen}} = \sum_{i=1}^N \mathbb{E} \int_0^T \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i^{\text{pen}}(t)^\top \frac{\partial \tilde{\mathbf{F}}_\psi}{\partial \mathbf{u}} \nabla_\psi \pi_\psi \, dt,
\tag{C.1}

where the augmented adjoint \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i^{\text{pen}} satisfies

\dot{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_i^{\text{pen}} = -\left(\frac{\partial \tilde{\mathbf{F}}_\psi}{\partial \mathbf{X}_i}\right)^\top \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i^{\text{pen}} - \sum_k \rho_k \, 2 \max(0, G_k) \, \frac{\partial G_k}{\partial \mathbf{X}_i},
\qquad \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i^{\text{pen}}(T) = \begin{pmatrix} \boldsymbol{\omega} \\ \mathbf{0} \end{pmatrix}.

The second term in the adjoint ODE accounts for the running penalty; note that it vanishes when constraints are satisfied. This expression is the exact gradient of the penalised objective, assuming the surrogate model is fixed.

C.3 Stochastic Gradient Ascent

The policy parameters \psi are updated using minibatch samples of learners:

\psi_{k+1} = \psi_k + \eta_k \hat{\nabla}_\psi J^{\text{pen}}_k,
\tag{C.2}

where \hat{\nabla}_\psi J^{\text{pen}}_k is an empirical estimate of (C.1) over a batch and the step sizes \eta_k satisfy the Robbins–Monro conditions \sum \eta_k = \infty, \sum \eta_k^2 < \infty.

Assumption C.1 (Smoothness). The penalised objective J^{\text{pen}}(\psi) is L‑smooth (gradient Lipschitz) and bounded above. The gradient estimator has bounded variance \mathbb{E} \|\hat{\nabla} - \nabla J^{\text{pen}}\|^2 \le \sigma^2.

Theorem C.1 (Convergence to a stationary point). Under Assumption C.1, the iterates (C.2) converge almost surely to a set where \nabla J^{\text{pen}}(\psi) = 0.

Proof. This is a direct application of the standard convergence theorem for stochastic gradient ascent on smooth functions (Bottou 1998, Theorem 2). The conditions on the step sizes and the gradient noise are satisfied. \blacksquare

C.4 From Stationary Points to Local Optimality

A stationary point of J^{\text{pen}} with sufficiently large penalty coefficients \rho_k corresponds to an approximate Karush–Kuhn–Tucker point of the original constrained problem. Because the neural policy class is a universal approximator, there exist network weights that make the policy arbitrarily close to any Lipschitz continuous feedback. A stationary point that satisfies the constraints with high accuracy is thus a local optimum of the original problem, provided the surrogate error \epsilon_{\text{sur}} is small enough. The precise statement is:

Revised Theorem 5.2 (Local convergence of neural policy gradient). Suppose the surrogate error is bounded by \epsilon_{\text{sur}} and the penalty coefficients are chosen such that \rho_k \to \infty as the training progresses (or are sufficiently large). Then any accumulation point of the policy gradient algorithm yields a policy that (a) satisfies the constraints (5.2)–(5.5) within a tolerance O(\epsilon_{\text{sur}}) and (b) is within O(\epsilon_{\text{sur}}) of a local optimum of the original DCAS‑OCP.

Proof sketch. The argument proceeds by showing that the penalised objective’s stationary points approximate the KKT points of the constrained problem. The surrogate error induces a perturbation in the dynamics, which by Lipschitz continuity propagates to an O(\epsilon_{\text{sur}}) deviation in the cost and constraints. Therefore, the policy obtained differs from a true local optimum by at most a constant multiple of \epsilon_{\text{sur}}. The limit of a decreasing sequence of such approximations (obtained by progressively increasing \rho_k and refining the surrogate) converges to a local optimum. \blacksquare

Remark on global optimality. If the Hamiltonian is strictly concave in \mathbf{u} for all admissible states (which may hold in the absence of the anti‑offloading constraint, or with a suitably convexified penalty), then every stationary point is a global maximiser. This provides a sufficient condition for global convergence, though it is not required for the practical success of the method.

C.5 Hierarchical Implementation

For planetary scales (N \sim 10^9), the gradient computation (C.1) is decoupled across macro‑groups. The hierarchical architecture described in Section 5.3.4 splits the problem into a small number of representative clusters. The global gradient is then a weighted average of cluster‑level gradients, each computed with a manageable batch size. This reduces per‑iteration complexity to O(K \cdot \text{batch\_size} \cdot m \cdot T) with K \ll N, making the algorithm feasible. The error introduced by clustering is bounded by the Lipschitz constant of the policy and the intra‑cluster variability of learner parameters, which can be controlled by refining the clustering.

