Appendix D: Proof of Proposition 5.1 — Equity‑Enhancing Control under Resource Saturation

Proposition 5.1 (Equity‑enhancing control). Let the population be partitioned into groups g = 1,\dots,G with mean cognitive capital C_g(t) = \frac{1}{N_g} \sum_{i \in g} \sum_j \omega_j h_{i,j}(t). Suppose that there exists a critical resource‑saturation threshold C_{\text{sat}} such that for any group with C_g(t) < C_{\text{sat}}, the marginal increase in learning rate per unit of additional pedagogical resource is strictly larger than for groups with C_g(t) \ge C_{\text{sat}}. Then there exists an admissible control policy that strictly increases the global average \bar{C}(t) while strictly decreasing the inter‑group variance \mathcal{I}(t) = \operatorname{Var}(C_1,\dots,C_G).

Proof. Without loss of generality, assume groups 1 and 2 are such that C_1(t) < C_{\text{sat}} \le C_2(t) and C_1(t) < \bar{C}(t) < C_2(t). Consider a small, total‑resource‑neutral reallocation of pedagogical effort from group 2 to group 1: \delta \mathbf{u}_{i} for a subset of learners. By the saturation assumption, the marginal gain in \dot{C}_1 exceeds the marginal loss in \dot{C}_2. Hence after an infinitesimal time interval, C_1(t+\delta t) + C_2(t+\delta t) > C_1(t) + C_2(t), raising the total capital, while the gap C_2 - C_1 narrows, reducing \mathcal{I}. The adjustment can be made smoothly along a trajectory, yielding a net improvement in both the average and the inequality measure over the horizon T.

The admissible control that achieves this is obtained by adding a small perturbation to the existing policy; since constraints are continuous and the original trajectory is in the interior of the feasible set (otherwise the group would be saturated), the perturbed control remains admissible for sufficiently small shifts. An iterative sequence of such reallocations monotonically improves the dual objective, and the limiting policy is Pareto‑optimal with respect to the equity–efficiency trade‑off. \blacksquare

Empirical note. The saturation threshold reflects that very low‑resource learners (e.g., those lacking basic literacy or numeric foundations) benefit enormously from structured instruction, whereas advanced learners operate near their individual cognitive speed limits. This is consistent with the well‑documented diminishing returns to educational investment at high skill levels (Hanushek & Woessmann 2015). For groups near the saturation threshold, Matthew effects may dominate, so the equity policy must be paired with careful monitoring; the assumption holds precisely for the groups DCAS targets for lifting.

