Appendix E: O‑Ring Microfoundations for the Cognitive Bottleneck Hypothesis

We present a simple formal model illustrating the threshold behaviour that underpins the CBH.

Consider a project P consisting of n independent tasks, each requiring a competence level at least r_v. The available workforce is a population of size N. An individual’s competence is a scalar c \in [0,1] drawn from a distribution F specific to the population. For each task v, a worker is competent with probability p_v = \mathbb{P}(c \ge r_v). The project fails if any task is assigned an incompetent worker; tasks are assigned without replacement.

The probability that there exists at least one fully competent team (i.e., a set of n distinct workers each competent for their assigned task) can be bounded. In the large‑N limit, the number of workers competent for task v is approximately Poisson with mean N p_v. The project succeeds if all n tasks can be simultaneously covered by disjoint workers. A necessary condition for success with high probability is that \min_v N p_v \ge n and in fact much larger if tasks are specialised. The success probability behaves roughly as

\mathbb{P}(\text{success}) \approx \exp\!\Bigl( - \sum_{v=1}^n e^{-N p_v} \Bigr),

for large N p_v. This function undergoes a sharp transition from near‑zero to near‑one as N increases past the point where the smallest p_v satisfies N p_v \gg \ln n. Hence a critical population size N_P^* emerges naturally from the complementarity of tasks.

When tasks are interdependent (O‑ring), the overall output is Q = \prod_{v=1}^n q(c_v), where q(c) is the task‑specific output function with q(c) \approx 0 if c < r_v. The expected output is then

\mathbb{E}[Q] = \prod_v \mathbb{E}[ q(c_v) \mid \text{assignment} ] \le \prod_v \bigl( \mathbb{P}(c_v \ge r_v) \, \bar{q}_v \bigr),

which again exhibits a threshold: if any task has \mathbb{P}(c_v \ge r_v) \ll 1, the whole product collapses. Thus, both task assignment models and O‑ring production functions confirm the central insight of the CBH: for highly complex, interdependent projects, the number of individuals with deep, integrated knowledge determines feasibility far more than material resources.

Relation to DCAS. The O‑ring logic implies that increasing the size of the qualified agent pool from below to above the threshold can have a discontinuous effect on civilisational capabilities. DCAS aims precisely to shift this pool beyond the critical thresholds for upcoming grand challenges, such as multiplanetary settlement.
